# Rust initial submission — ABI v1

Implemented a normal-priority service with four encoding workers and baseline
one-job admission/terminal WriteBatches. No ABI v2, group commit, alternate
kernel, service test hooks, or durability substitution. Source, executable,
build script, tests and experiment record are delivered in this workspace.

## Build and run

Only through the supplied Linux broker:

```sh
python3 run.py './build.sh' 300
python3 run.py './tests/run.sh' 300
python3 run.py './service DATADIR SOCKET_PATH'
```

`service` is a Linux AArch64 ELF executable. It dynamically imports
`rn_verify_v1` from `/adapter/librosetta.so` and the designated RocksDB C API
from `librocksdb.so.7.8`; runtime search path is `/adapter`. The root Cargo.lock
matches reuse/Cargo.lock byte for byte. Dependency versions/checksums are unchanged.
`tmp/final-artifacts.json` records the final executable and source hashes.

## Source and ownership

- `src/main.rs`: single state/storage owner; recovery, durable admissions and
  cancellations, four workers, admission-ordered terminal commitment and drain.
- `src/storage.rs`: supplied dynamic C wrapper, extended with a checked iterator
  and null DB-handle check. DB and batches remain on their creating thread;
  no unsafe Send/Sync implementations. Required cache/buffer/compression settings,
  WAL and synchronous writes are preserved.
- `src/native.rs`: ABI v1 layouts and arena RAII. Workers create and destroy their
  own arenas; source bytes and native input/result arrays remain alive through
  verification. Cancellation never interrupts/freezes/frees a worker's arena.
- `src/protocol.rs`: strict JSON/hex/ID validation, wire/storage shapes and limits.
- `src/hash.rs`: standalone general SHA-256 solely for job identity. Transaction
  parsing and both transaction digests remain entirely in the common adapter.
- `src/transport.rs`: eight live connections maximum, one reader and writer each.
  Frames are bounded before buffering beyond 40 MiB. Each reader waits for its
  response to finish writing. Thirty-two best-effort event slots plus one reserved
  response slot bound output and preserve admission-response/event ordering.
  Socket writes have a two-second timeout and never block the state owner.

Admission persists the complete job/index/accounting before acknowledgment.
Cancellation persists intent before acknowledgment. The same owner serializes
terminal C write calls and cancellation decisions, so acknowledged cancellation
before terminal call entry wins. Running cancellations wait for native return;
cancelled queued work can terminalize without entering the kernel. Terminal
commit removes pending/cancel records, writes the receipt, advances checkpoint
and releases accounting atomically. The ID index and receipts remain forever.

Startup validates schema, record/index bijection, contiguous checkpoint, pending
payload digests, cancellation membership and accounting; inconsistent state fails
explicitly rather than rebuilding. Absent-version initialization requires an empty
database and durably writes version and all counters together. Exclusive ownership
is RocksDB's native LOCK. Storage errors and acknowledged-job arena failures stop
the process without acknowledging/terminalizing the failed transition.

## Validation

Final `tests/run.sh` passed: one Rust test covering four published SHA-256 vectors,
the public smoke test, and all 12 black-box Python integration tests (7.382 seconds
for the last integration run). `cargo clippy --offline --locked -- -D warnings`
and pinned `cargo fmt` also passed. Artifact audit checks ELF architecture, execute
permission, required dynamic imports/runpath, v1-only verifier and unchanged lock.

Integration coverage includes:

- Direct comparison with the supplied adapter: 64-item mixed batches, malformed
  items, empty bytes, prefix/exact, both modes and per-item resource limits.
- Invalid JSON/UTF-8/fields/IDs/hex, batch count, 40 MiB framing and partial EOF;
  rejected inputs do not change durable state; pipelined response ordering.
- Digest identity, conflict/retry, response-before-event, retained receipts and
  restart, missing status/cancel without rows, native exclusive writer lock.
- Eight clients admitting 160 jobs, live cancellation, disconnect, idle/slow
  readers, and zero-exit draining shutdown.
- Three crashes with 16 MiB jobs, confirmed acknowledged pending recovery and
  accepted cancellations; five timed process crashes during request streams.
- Independent stopped-writer RocksDB inspection of every checkpoint/pending/
  receipt/index/cancel/accounting invariant after crashes and shutdown.
- Valid recovery fixtures containing durable cancellation and a full 1,024-job,
  64 MiB backlog; backpressure, idempotent retry and rejection of new work during
  drain. Incompatible schema is rejected without replacing stored records.

Tests use Python standard library, ctypes and supplied native interfaces only.
They add no scheduling or barrier hooks to the service. All builds, linting,
artifact inspection and test execution used `python3 run.py`.

## Assumptions and remaining evidence

Unknown request/item fields are rejected as reserved. Missing/non-string request
correlation returns invalid_request with an empty request string. Overlong or
unterminated frames receive invalid_request when possible and close the connection.
The 40 MiB text bound excludes newline. Empty/over-64 batches and aggregate input
over 16 MiB are invalid_request; item-level kernel resource errors remain results.
V1 accepts omitted priority or explicit normal, rejects high. Existing/conflicting
IDs retain their retry disposition during drain; new work receives draining.
These choices resolve ambiguities in the supplied prose without user questions.

No evaluator, external benchmark, gate/freeze artifact, native ASan variant,
whole-service race detector or systematic write/arena failure injector was accessed.
Tests establish sampled process-crash recovery, not power-loss or exhaustive
interleaving correctness. Native allocation failure and storage-error branches
were reviewed but not fault-injected. The test backlog demonstrates bounded
admission and draining; it is not a comparative performance measurement.

No predecessor handoff was present. The user explicitly authorized implementation
after reading; the preregistration's preparation/freeze language remains external
campaign context, not evidence of gate execution. Keep v1 and its durable schema
intact for the next round; v2 has not been implemented in anticipation.

